nodecode-basekit is a rewrite of D0xedDevi0's D0xedDevi0/hermes-basekit:

  https://github.com/D0xedDevi0/hermes-basekit
  commit 142bd48ea7755bda6a7c2aff707106bdfbf25ee6
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 D0XEDDEV (LICENSE)

The plugin was a Hermes plugin: six agent tools (base_network, base_portfolio,
base_tx, base_token, base_activity, base_allowances) over a stdlib-only Python
read engine, plus a pre_llm_call hook, a /basekit slash command, a `hermes
basekit` CLI command, a desktop pane, and a bundled skill. This rewrite keeps
the six tools, their names and their arguments, and the /basekit command's
sub-commands, and reimplements the reads in Common Lisp against the endpoints
the original named: Base JSON-RPC (keyless public endpoints with the same
failover list), DefiLlama's keyless price API, Coinbase's spot ETH price, and
Etherscan V2 only when a Basescan key is set. The engine's verified constants
are re-used unchanged - the ERC-20 selectors, the Transfer and Approval topic
hashes, the EIP-1967 storage slots, the EIP-1167 clone prefix, the selector
probe list, and the curated token set from tokens.json. What was left out: the
pre_llm_call hook (a cell's round hook does not see the user's message here,
so the same guidance is the manual topic and the tool descriptions), the JSONL
ledger, the CLI command and the desktop pane and status chip (Hermes
surfaces), the automatic halving of a too-wide log window (a rejected window
is one error line here, and the two log tools state the window they read), the
TTL caches on RPC reads and prices, and the Multicall3 batching, which one
JSON-RPC batch replaces with the same round-trip count. Nothing of the original
is vendored; no Python, Node or binary is bundled.
